<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Deliberately NOT sandboxed, unlike the Team-signed build (which keeps
	  DropDrive.entitlements and its full sandbox intact).

	  GoogleSignIn stores its session in the keychain, and a sandboxed app can't
	  reach the keychain at all without a keychain-access-groups entitlement.
	  That entitlement is only honoured when it matches a real Apple-issued Team
	  prefix: the Team-signed build uses "$(AppIdentifierPrefix)…", which ad-hoc
	  signing cannot resolve. Both self-assigned variants were tried and both
	  make launchd refuse to spawn the app outright (RBSRequestError 5 /
	  POSIX 163):
	    - keychain-access-groups = com.dropdrive.DropDrive
	    - the same plus com.apple.application-identifier
	  Leaving the entitlement out instead lets the app launch, but then every
	  sign-in dies with GID "keychain error" (code -2) the moment the OAuth
	  redirect succeeds — the "it asks me to connect while already connected"
	  bug.

	  So under ad-hoc signing it's sandbox or keychain, not both. This build is
	  distributed as a DMG rather than through the App Store, where the sandbox
	  is optional, so it's dropped here to make sign-in work at all. The
	  remaining sandbox permissions (network.client, files.user-selected) are
	  meaningless outside the sandbox and are omitted. The Share extension stays
	  sandboxed — app extensions must be, and it never touches the keychain.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
